          "The four types by mechanism: **vertigo** (illusion of movement - vestibular), **presyncope** (about to faint - global cerebral hypoperfusion), **disequilibrium** (unsteady on the feet - sensorimotor balance failure) and **non-specific lightheadedness** (floating, woozy - anxiety, hyperventilation, drugs). [Harrison 22e]",
          "The classification examiners now expect is **TiTrATE (Timing, Triggers And Targeted Examination)**, which groups patients into **acute vestibular syndrome, triggered episodic, spontaneous episodic and chronic** dizziness, each with benign and dangerous causes - see the table. [GRACE-3 2023]",
          "Do **not ask what it feels like** - patients describe quality inconsistently; timing (onset, duration of each episode) and triggers (head position, standing, none) are reproducible and diagnostic. [GRACE-3 2023]",
          "Vertigo is further split into **peripheral** (labyrinth or vestibular nerve - BPPV, vestibular neuritis, Meniere disease) and **central** (brainstem or cerebellum - stroke, vestibular migraine, demyelination, tumour). [Harrison 22e]",
